WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.640
100 Starlings from 1890 now number, 150 million, and cost farms, 800 million dollars a year. This is Unintended Consequences, Episode 167. What follows is the society that wanted familiar European birds in American parks. The two quiet releases in Central Park, the path from city trees to grain and orchards, the native nesters and municipal roosts that came with that spread, the management that never restored the old numbers, and the review date that was never set.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:37.097
The American Aclimatization Society was founded in New York in 1871. Its purpose was to introduce

00:00:37.097 --> 00:00:43.177
foreign birds and mammals that members judged beneficial or ornamental. Eugene Shefelin, a member of

00:00:43.177 --> 00:00:50.737
that society, arranged releases of European starlings in Central Park in 1890 and again in 1891 as part

00:00:50.737 --> 00:00:56.517
of that broader program. At the time, many civic groups treated deliberate acclimatization as a

00:00:56.517 --> 00:00:59.177
straightforward way to improve landscapes and agriculture.

00:00:59.897 --> 00:01:04.617
The society's stated goal was to enrich North American wildlife with familiar and useful

00:01:04.617 --> 00:01:05.737
old-world species.

00:01:06.457 --> 00:01:11.677
European starlings were already known in their native range as adaptable insectivores that

00:01:11.677 --> 00:01:12.997
also ate fruit and grain.

00:01:13.797 --> 00:01:17.537
No contemporary records show a Shakespearean motive for choosing the starling.

00:01:18.017 --> 00:01:24.177
That story first appeared in a 1948 essay by Edwin Way Teal, and researchers John McNeil

00:01:24.177 --> 00:01:27.817
Miller and Lauren Fugate have since traced it to a later invention.

00:01:28.377 --> 00:01:33.277
The society acted on a simpler assumption, a hearty, familiar songbird would settle

00:01:33.277 --> 00:01:35.337
without disrupting existing systems.

00:01:35.897 --> 00:01:40.557
Members reason that if the birds followed patterns observed in Europe, they would eat insects

00:01:40.557 --> 00:01:43.317
during the breeding season and only limited fruit later.

00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:48.537
The hoped-for result was more variety in city parks, with commercial agriculture left

00:01:48.537 --> 00:01:49.637
largely untouched.

00:01:49.637 --> 00:01:54.277
The original plan assumed the birds would stay inside the bounds of an aesthetic benefit,

00:01:54.697 --> 00:01:56.777
or at most a minor ecological one.

00:01:57.237 --> 00:02:01.857
Nothing in the process required anyone to model long-term population growth, or to consult

00:02:01.857 --> 00:02:03.677
farmers before the birds went free.

00:02:04.257 --> 00:02:08.937
The decision rested entirely on the visible success of earlier, smaller introductions of

00:02:08.937 --> 00:02:09.597
other species.

00:02:10.257 --> 00:02:14.897
From where the members stood, a small release of a familiar bird looked like a finished improvement,

00:02:15.317 --> 00:02:17.557
not the opening of a continental experiment.

00:02:17.557 --> 00:02:23.109
The society freed the birds in two successive years inside the wooded sections of Central

00:02:23.109 --> 00:02:23.429
Park.

00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:28.249
The release itself was a deliberate small-scale action in a single urban park.

00:02:28.849 --> 00:02:32.789
Early observers noted that the starlings nested successfully in the city's buildings

00:02:32.789 --> 00:02:33.349
and trees.

00:02:34.029 --> 00:02:37.169
Those first birds did not need special food or special housing.

00:02:37.629 --> 00:02:42.629
They used eaves, vents, and tree cavities already present in the urban environment, so

00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:45.289
their initial survival did not depend on provisioning.

00:02:45.289 --> 00:02:50.669
Once a few pairs raised young inside the park, the next generation dispersed along rail

00:02:50.669 --> 00:02:54.749
lines and river corridors and reached agricultural counties within a decade.

00:02:55.249 --> 00:02:59.969
By the early 20th century, small flocks had already moved into surrounding farmland.

00:03:00.449 --> 00:03:05.249
The releases sat alongside other society efforts that had succeeded with house sparrows and

00:03:05.249 --> 00:03:09.449
other species, and that record reinforced the sense that the approach worked.

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:14.249
Within decades, the birds had spread across the continent and begun feeding on ripening

00:03:14.249 --> 00:03:19.209
grain and orchard fruit in volumes that existing farm practices could not easily offset.

00:03:19.889 --> 00:03:24.429
No formal monitoring program tracked the Starling's first movements beyond the city limits.

00:03:25.109 --> 00:03:29.949
The society recorded the releases as completed tasks rather than as the start of an ongoing

00:03:29.949 --> 00:03:30.649
experiment.

00:03:31.209 --> 00:03:35.289
So no one went back to the original numbers or compared them with later observations

00:03:35.289 --> 00:03:36.129
of flock size.

00:03:36.689 --> 00:03:41.789
Because the birds adapt to buildings, open fields, and livestock operations alike, their

00:03:41.789 --> 00:03:45.929
foraging reached crops that had never before faced this particular pressure at scale.

00:03:46.865 --> 00:03:51.005
The starlings proved far more mobile and prolific than the society anticipated.

00:03:51.505 --> 00:03:55.545
By most accounts, and according to data from the United States Department of Agriculture

00:03:55.545 --> 00:04:00.445
Wildlife Services, the population across North America had reached that continental

00:04:00.445 --> 00:04:02.405
scale by the late 20th century.

00:04:03.065 --> 00:04:07.605
The bird's subsequent movements turned a localized introduction into a continental presence.

00:04:07.605 --> 00:04:13.425
Their mobility and their diet produced measurable losses that accumulated year after year on distant

00:04:13.425 --> 00:04:14.525
farms and orchards.

00:04:15.085 --> 00:04:18.885
In winter they form large flocks that descend on orchards and grain fields.

00:04:19.345 --> 00:04:23.885
They damage apples, cherries, grapes, and blueberries by pecking and slashing fruit.

00:04:24.625 --> 00:04:28.185
Damage fruit then rots or becomes vulnerable to insects and disease.

00:04:28.805 --> 00:04:33.445
In concentrated animal feeding operations they consume livestock feed and contaminate

00:04:33.445 --> 00:04:36.345
it, which creates losses beyond the fruit and the grain.

00:04:36.345 --> 00:04:41.565
seasonal control such as netting or shooting produced only local, temporary relief

00:04:41.565 --> 00:04:46.525
because the birds adapt quickly to new food sources and new nesting sites. When

00:04:46.525 --> 00:04:50.325
one orchardist installed netting, neighboring operations without the same

00:04:50.325 --> 00:04:54.585
investment still lost fruit, and the birds simply shifted to the next available

00:04:54.585 --> 00:04:59.385
field. The same adaptability that let the species thrive in cities also let it

00:04:59.385 --> 00:05:03.345
exploit every new crop variety and feeding system that agriculture developed

00:05:03.345 --> 00:05:04.525
up after 1900.

00:05:05.385 --> 00:05:07.785
Second order effects reached beyond the crop rows.

00:05:08.405 --> 00:05:11.865
Starlings competed with native cavity nesting birds for nest holes.

00:05:12.465 --> 00:05:17.785
Large urban roosts concentrated in cities and required separate municipal responses, which

00:05:17.785 --> 00:05:21.125
became a standing cost for places that had never asked for the birds.

00:05:21.785 --> 00:05:26.845
Over time, the cumulative effect on fruit quality and grain weight grew large enough that programs

00:05:26.845 --> 00:05:31.965
at the United States Department of Agriculture began treating starling damage as a standing

00:05:31.965 --> 00:05:37.305
line item rather than an occasional nuisance. The original aesthetic and agricultural intentions

00:05:37.305 --> 00:05:42.905
therefore generated persistent, continent-wide costs that no single farmer or orchardist

00:05:42.905 --> 00:05:48.285
could prevent. A park release had become a pressure on crops, on native nesters, and on

00:05:48.285 --> 00:05:52.985
city budgets, and none of those later costs had a single owner who could turn the introduction

00:05:52.985 --> 00:05:58.510
off. Federal and state agencies later built targeted management programs through the United

00:05:58.510 --> 00:06:03.790
United States Department of Agriculture Animal and Plant Health Inspection Service, APHIS,

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:05.350
Wildlife Services.

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:11.170
Those programs include Habitat Modification, Chemical Repelance, and Limited Lethal Measures

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:12.370
at High Damage Sites.

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:16.990
None of them has reduced the continental population to pre-1900 levels.

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:22.910
Some growers shifted to earlier or later ripening varieties, and some adopted exclusion netting.

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:25.370
Both measures add production costs.

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:29.830
States. Growers who adopted netting early found that maintenance and seasonal installation

00:06:29.830 --> 00:06:35.170
became recurring expenses folded into annual budgets. Operations that could not afford the

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:37.990
same infrastructure continued to absorb direct losses.

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:43.190
The episode also prompted later scrutiny of other deliberate introductions, though the

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:47.830
legal and cultural acceptance of such releases had already declined for unrelated regulatory

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:53.830
reasons. Today the styling remains a widespread resident. Its presence is treated as a permanent

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:56.630
management challenge rather than a reversible mistake.

00:06:57.070 --> 00:07:01.350
The tools that emerged addressed symptoms at individual sites and left the underlying

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:03.670
population size and range unchanged.

00:07:04.746 --> 00:07:09.526
When the American Aclimatization Society released those birds without a documented plan for

00:07:09.526 --> 00:07:15.286
monitoring spread or crop impact, the choice rewarded an assumption that a single attractive

00:07:15.286 --> 00:07:17.546
species would stay inside intended bounds.

00:07:18.126 --> 00:07:22.666
What was measured, if anything was measured at all, was whether the release had been carried

00:07:22.666 --> 00:07:27.546
out and whether the birds nested in the park. What was not measured was how far the next

00:07:27.546 --> 00:07:32.466
generation traveled or what ripening fruit and stored feed would lose once flocks arrived.

00:07:33.226 --> 00:07:38.226
Early success with house sparrows made that missing measurement feel unnecessary. Small early

00:07:38.226 --> 00:07:43.586
numbers could become large later ones without anyone adjusting course, because no feedback mechanism

00:07:43.586 --> 00:07:49.326
sat between the release and the farms. Decision makers who evaluate new introductions today can

00:07:49.326 --> 00:07:54.706
require explicit thresholds for population growth and documented damage before any release

00:07:54.706 --> 00:07:59.886
begins. Anyone weighing a similar landscape change can ask what measurable signal would

00:07:59.886 --> 00:08:04.026
prompt reversal if the first two years of results differ from the original projection.

00:08:04.626 --> 00:08:10.286
A practical step is to tie any future introduction to a pre-agreed review date, at which measured

00:08:10.286 --> 00:08:15.446
outcomes rather than initial hopes determine whether the project continues. The same gap

00:08:15.446 --> 00:08:20.006
shows up whenever a hearty organism is added to a working landscape because it is familiar,

00:08:20.626 --> 00:08:25.786
useful, or simply pleasant to have nearby. And the only checkpoint is the day it is set loose.

00:08:26.606 --> 00:08:30.706
Environmental intelligence spends its days on the regulatory side of living systems,

00:08:31.106 --> 00:08:35.426
which is where a threshold like that would have to be written down before the first pair nests.

00:08:36.106 --> 00:08:41.046
If a city, an agency, or a grower cooperative were about to introduce a species for ornament

00:08:41.046 --> 00:08:46.086
or for pest control, what number on what date would be enough to stop the project while

00:08:46.086 --> 00:08:50.706
stopping was still possible. We'll leave it there for today. Thanks for thinking through

00:08:50.706 --> 00:08:54.886
this one with me. If you know someone who loves a good cautionary tale, send it their

